# Anything executed by a POSIX shell MUST check out LF, on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so without this a Windows
# clone gets CRLF working-tree copies of these files — and `docker build` (the
# documented build-from-source path) then bakes a CRLF entrypoint into the
# image. The container dies the instant it starts, with:
#
#     env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# which names neither the file nor the cause. Linux/macOS clones and CI never
# reproduce it, so it is invisible until a Windows user tries to build.
# Measured on a real Windows 10 box: `docker build` succeeded and the container
# exited 127 immediately.
#
# `text eol=lf` is deliberate over `-text`: these files are still text (diffs,
# merges), they simply must never gain CRLF in the working tree.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.command text eol=lf
docker/entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
docker/caddy-sites.sh text eol=lf
# Read inside the Linux container by Caddy.
Caddyfile* text eol=lf
docker/Caddyfile text eol=lf

# One-click Windows wrapper files ship inside a ZIP and are parsed by
# cmd.exe/Notepad: they MUST stay CRLF byte-for-byte. Mark them -text so git
# never re-normalizes them on any platform. Listed AFTER the rules above so it
# wins for anything under this path (last matching rule takes precedence).
packaging/one-click/windows/** -text
# .url is a Windows INI file inside the MSI payload: keep CRLF byte-for-byte.
packaging/msi/*.url -text
